-- CA_0317_la_council_district_current_map.sql
-- CA_0317: Los Angeles city council districts -- replace the shipped 2012-adopted boundaries with the
--   current (post-2021-redistricting) map, for all 15 districts.
--
-- DEFECT: essentials.geofence_boundaries held 15 rows tagged source='la_city_geohub_council_districts_2024'
--   whose polygons were in fact the 2012-adopted map. backend/scripts/load-la-city-council-boundaries.ts
--   pointed at a third-party ArcGIS Online copy whose data was last edited 2021-03-22; the "_2024" in the
--   tag was the year the loader ran, not the vintage of the data. Reported by a resident of 1338 N Ogden Dr,
--   who was shown Nithya Raman (CD4) when the ballot authority puts that address in CD13 (Hugo Soto-Martinez).
--
-- BLAST RADIUS BEFORE THE FIX: on a 1,486-point ~900m grid inside the city limits, 91 cells (6.1%) resolved
--   to the wrong councilmember. The same grid run between the 2012 and current maps differs on 90 cells with
--   an identical confusion matrix (4->5: 29, 5->4: 14, 4->2: 14, 13->4: 10, 14->1: 5, 2->4: 5, 4->12: 5,
--   4->3: 3), which is what identified the shipped data AS the 2012 map. The 91st cell is a boundary sliver
--   that resolved to no district at all. Area-weighted, not population-weighted.
--
-- WHY NO CHECK CAUGHT IT: backend/scripts/check-la-council-bounds.ts only asserts the rows EXIST. The stale
--   upstream also carried a councilmember name per polygon, and every name was a sitting member, so a roster
--   cross-check passed. Only a geometry comparison against an independent authority exposes a stale boundary.
--   This migration ships backend/scripts/check-council-district-vintage.mjs (npm run check:district-vintage)
--   to close that gap for every X-layer council map, not just LA.
--
-- SOURCE / VINTAGE PROOF (also written to geofence_boundaries.source):
--   City of Los Angeles, NavigateLA Boundaries MapServer layer 13 "Council Districts"
--   (maps.lacity.org/lahub/rest/services/Boundaries/MapServer/13), read 2026-10-20. This REPLACES the 2012-adopted
--   map these rows previously held, which came from the third-party AGOL copy
--   services5.arcgis.com/7nsPwEMP38bSkCjy Council_Districts/FeatureServer/0 (editingInfo.lastEditDate 2021-03-22,
--   months before the current map took effect) and was mis-tagged la_city_geohub_council_districts_2024. VINTAGE
--   PROVED against the LA County Registrar-Recorder precinct layer (public.gis.lacounty.gov Political_Boundaries
--   MapServer 34, DST_CITY=LS / DIV_CITY) -- the layer the county builds BALLOTS from, the Columbus arbiter rule
--   -- on 25 of 25 sampled cells where the 2012 and current maps disagree: 25 agree with THIS layer, 0 with the
--   superseded one. The layer NAME field agrees with the sitting council 15/15 and is recorded as CORROBORATION
--   ONLY, never as the vintage proof (an officeholder name can be current while the boundary is stale -- that is
--   exactly how the 2012 map passed unnoticed: it carried "Nithya Raman", a sitting member).
--
-- Geometry is the layer's own, reprojected to EPSG:4326 by the service and rounded to 7 decimal places
--   (~1cm); no simplification, no make_valid rewriting -- all 15 arrive valid.
-- Rows are UPDATEd in place (matched on ocd_id), so geofence_boundaries.id is preserved.
-- Apply with: npx tsx scripts/_apply-file.ts <abs path>   (geometry-heavy; do not paste through MCP).

BEGIN;

-- District 1 (Eunisses Hernandez)
